### Why clamp negative gains to zero?

If a child's gain is negative, including it would drag the total down. Clamping to zero effectively excludes that branch. This means a path can start or end at any node without being forced to include a loss-making subtree.

```java
// Without clamping: negative branch drags down the total
int leftGain = gain(node.left); // could be -10
int total = node.profit + leftGain; // reduced by 10!

// With clamping: negative branch excluded
int leftGain = Math.max(gain(node.left), 0); // at worst 0
int total = node.profit + leftGain; // not reduced
```

### Why initialize to Integer.MIN_VALUE instead of zero?

If all node values are negative, the answer is the single least-negative node. Initializing to zero would incorrectly return zero when the real answer is a negative number.

```java
// WRONG: returns 0 when all values are negative
int maxSum = 0;

// CORRECT: handles all-negative trees
int maxSum = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

// Example: tree with nodes [-3, -5, -1]
// Correct answer: -1 (the single least-negative node)
```

### Why does the return differ from the global update?

The return value feeds a straight path upward through one child for the parent to extend. The global update captures the complete bent path that turns at this node and cannot be extended further. These are fundamentally different quantities.

```java
// Return: upward contribution (one child only)
return node.profit + Math.max(leftGain, rightGain);

// Global: bending path (both children)
maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, node.profit + leftGain + rightGain);

// A path going up to the parent cannot fork, so return uses one child.
// The best path that turns here uses both children but stops.
```

### What are the edge cases?

Single node, all negative values, and a node with one child.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.ProfitNode;

public class MaxPathEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MaxPathSum solver = new MaxPathSum();

        // Single node
        System.out.println("Single: " + solver.maxPathSum(new ProfitNode("A", 5)));

        // All negative
        ProfitNode neg = new ProfitNode("A", -3);
        neg.left = new ProfitNode("B", -5);
        neg.right = new ProfitNode("C", -1);
        System.out.println("All negative: " + solver.maxPathSum(neg));
    }
}
```

```text
Single: 5
All negative: -1
```

### How is this related to the diameter problem?

Both problems share the same recursive structure: a post-order helper that returns one value to the parent while updating a global with a different value. In diameter, you return depth and update `leftDepth + rightDepth`. Here, you return upward gain and update the bending path sum.

```java
// Shared pattern:
private int helper(Node node) {
    if (node == null) return 0;
    int left = helper(node.left);
    int right = helper(node.right);
    globalMax = Math.max(globalMax, COMBINE(left, right, node.val));
    return PROPAGATE(left, right, node.val);
}
// Diameter:   COMBINE = left + right, PROPAGATE = 1 + max(left, right)
// Max Path:   COMBINE = val + left + right, PROPAGATE = val + max(left, right)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Explaining precisely why the return and the global differ is what convinces an interviewer of genuine understanding. The return feeds a straight continuation upward; the global captures the best turning point. This distinction is the heart of the solution.

In production at National Bank of India, this models a chain of internal cost centers where each carries a net profit or loss. Finance wants the most profitable connected chain. Loss-making centers are excluded by the clamp-to-zero step, and the best chain often bends at a mid-level center rather than going through the top.

```java
// Production usage: find most profitable chain in cost center tree
int bestChain = new MaxPathSum().maxPathSum(costCenterRoot);
reportService.submit("Most profitable chain sum: " + bestChain);
```
